  "generated_text": "1. The total number of people surveyed is 472.\n2. The central angle of the \"Pop\" sector is \\(251^\\circ\\).\n3. To find the number of people who chose \"Pop,\" we need to determine the fraction of the total population that chose \"Pop.\"\n\nThe fraction of the total population that chose \"Pop\" is given by the ratio of the central angle of the \"Pop\" sector to the total central angle of the pie chart (which is \\(360^\\circ\\)):\n\n\\[\n\\text{Fraction} = \\frac{251^\\circ}{360^\\circ}\n\\]\n\n4. Simplify the fraction:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Fraction} = \\frac{251}{360} = \\frac{17}{24}\n\\]\n\n5. Now, multiply the total number of people surveyed by this fraction to find the number of people who chose \"Pop\":\n\n\\[\n\\text{Number of people} = 472 \\times \\frac{17}{24}\n\\]\n\n6. Perform the multiplication:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{34}\n\\]",
  "grade": {
    "error": null,
    "extracted_answer": "[34, '34']",
    "gold_answer": "We want to find the fraction of people that chose ``Pop\". To this end, we find the fraction of the circle that represents ``Pop\", $\\frac{251}{360}$, and multiply by the total number of people surveyed: $472 \\cdot \\frac{251}{360} \\approx 329.089$. Rounding to a whole number gives a possible answer of $329$ people.\n\nThis method doesn't prove that the answer is unique, but we can check that $328$ people would take up only $\\frac{328}{472}\\cdot 360 \\approx 250.169$ degrees of the pie chart, while $330$ people would take up $\\frac{330}{472}\\cdot 360 \\approx 251.695$ degrees. So, $\\boxed{329}$ people are the only number whose share of the pie rounds to the nearest degree as $251^\\circ$.",
    "is_correct": false
  },
